commentBody:
@SP 
Me too.  Maybe actually even more often.  Like Hazel F, I met LOTR when no longer a kid.  I'd had some linguistics and some northern European history and literature studies, and Tolkien just captured my brain and stuck there. 

And I'll second  @Sara W's suggestion-- Master and Commander Series as audio books kept me entertained and sane for several years of hour+ commutes to/from work.

@Hazel F
As long as we're recommending book series,I'll chime in with Philip Pullman's "His Dark Materials". In some ways an atheist's counter to LOTR.
  And I try to keep in mind  the advice at the end of the last book, when two characters agree they must never meet again (would be a spoiler if I explained why) but will take times to remember each other and what they've learned: "Be cheerful and not surly."

@SP
I agree with most of your assessment, but the Jordan clue a misdirection? I found it very straightforward. 
Guess that's a YMMV.

@SP
I first thought Jordan was a forgotten character in the Orwell novel (last read in maybe 1974).  Wasn't there an Anti-Sex League in there? ASL? No?
Didn't last long.

@DAH
There's a book of pop-anthropology titled *A History of the World in 6 Glasses*, by Tom Standage (2005), which discusses six different beverages, and their roles in shaping civilization. They are, in order, beer, wine, distilled liquors (particularly rum), coffee, tea, and Coca-cola. In the book's cover artwork, images of the six beverages are worked--the word "the" in the title is replaced by a canister of tea. 

It's a great read--I highly recommend it!

@RL

Absolutely as @HeathieJ says - just take a break and go back to it later, don't let it frustrate you!
I completed most of this puzzle, fairly slowly but never totally stuck, but then hit a brick wall in the north section. Just couldn't budge any of the final 5 or 6 clues I had left. I came back to it after a sleep, and bam! everything fell into place! That's often even more satisfying than just filling in a puzzle easily :)

@sotto voce
Isn't your phone a wee computer?  
Quite possibly I am just ignorant.

@Down_Home

You're welcome!

The theme seemed awfully pedestrian until I noticed that.  If Adrianne had gotten herself into a spot, she couldn't just pull one out and replace it with another random computer phrase.  That made me appreciate the rest of the fill even more.

@Xword Junkie

My Grampa would plow a field, then go back over it with the HARROW attachment.  (His tractor did many tasks that once were accomplished by hand.)  Which came first?  The tool or the other meanings?  
Quite apt, eh?

@Mean Old Lady

A quick look suggests that HARROW denoting the tool was first recorded around 1300, while the usage in today's puzzle arose much later.

Thanks for asking!

@Xword Junkie

From Wikipedia:

The word harrow originally comes from the Old English hergian 'to harry or despoil', and is seen in the homilies of Ælfric of Eynsham, c. 1000.[b] The term Harrowing of Hell refers not merely to the idea that Jesus descended into Hell, as in the Creed, but to the rich tradition that developed later, asserting that he triumphed over inferos, releasing Hell's captives, particularly Adam and Eve, and the righteous men and women of the Old Testament period.

@Nancy
🙌 I would. As a late Boomer I too hanker after pre digital days. Not being available to all and sundry 24/7 would be bliss. Looking at the world rather than at a screen is a vanishing ability. 
I was in a packed hospital waiting room a while ago, (sick Mum, all fine now). The entire 5 and a half hours we were there everyone but Mum and I were glued to their phones, we were the only ones out of around 40 people who had brought a book. I know, what a revolution. Nurses had to call out names several times before the zombies could tear themselves away from their screens to respond. Sadly, this covered all ages, so we can’t roll our eyes at the young.

@Nancy

You inspired me! You'll be thrilled to know I had AI write that book.

It spent 3.14 seconds at the top of the best-seller list, before being overtaken by the newer, lustier "Fifty Shades of Plaintive Voices From the 20th Century".

@Nancy
I recently gave a series of six one-hour classes. No Zooming, just human beings in a room. (I did record the audio for those who had to miss one class or another.) I can't count how many people began their emails with "Thank God!"
As a teacher, I can tell you that trying to teach a little box on a screen is Sisyphean. Distance learning is aptly named.

@Jeanne @Helen Wright 
And if the NHS could do it, they'd miss the people who brought books!
 Me, since I'm a Libra (we don't believe in astrology but are all in for compromise), I usually have BOTH a book AND the puzzles on my phone.

@Nancy
Boook? What is boook?

Late boomer trying to feel the zeitgeist.

@Grant
As Alice Roosevelt Longworth is reported to have said, "If you can't think of anything nice to say, come and sit by me."
Really, if you don't enjoy a baseball game, ask a friend who loves the game to accompany you and explain it as it unfolds before you. Ask lots of questions including the dreaded "What just happened?", don't be afraid to look dumb, and strike up a conversation with the stranger behind you. It's a fascinating and beautiful game if you give it a chance.

@mike
Whatever you may feel about gender, sexuality, and the politics thereof, naming things is just useful from the point of view of discussion. And if it's a name, it's a word. And if it's a word, it can be in a crossword.
Besides, this lets you fill in the blank on your  "Down With _____" placard.

@Xword Junkie. Great stuff thanks!   The sequence itself might have been described by mathematicians in India much earlier than the Europeans.   

Always noteworthy that 56% of Americans oppose the teaching of Arabic numerals in school.

And if Americans knew most of the stars had Arabic names, might be an executive order renaming those stars.

@sonnel and @Captain Quahog
 I share your politics but I fear that both those stories are "too good to check". As they say on Wikipedia: "citation needed".
  But prove me wrong if you can.

@RozzieGrandma - my comment was thoroughly in jest, although I bet the percentage of people who would oppose it is non-zero, and higher than we would hope. 

However, sonnel's comment is based on actual survey results. 

<a href="https://www.the-independent.com/news/arabic-numerals-survey-prejudice-bias-survey-research-civic-science-a8918256.html" target="_blank">https://www.the-independent.com/news/arabic-numerals-survey-prejudice-bias-survey-research-civic-science-a8918256.html</a>

@Helen Wright
I just watched what Garron Noone (hilarious Irish guy) had to say about it:
“It’s all clouds. Irish solar eclipse, happens every fn day”.

@Helen Wright

Two years ago, we were supposed to have around 90% coverage where I live, so I drove four hours to close to the Canadian border to see totality. It was quite worth it, but folks back home were not impressed by what they were able to see. 

It took nine hours to drive back, as everyone left at the same time, and there were only two lanes going home!

@Myra

The Best Picture award was in 2025, but the film was released in 2024. And the clue identified it as a 2024 film - not the year of the award. 

